libcompanion/libcompanion/minhook/

  MinHook, the minimalistic x86/x64 API hooking library for Windows.

  Origin:   https://github.com/TsudaKageyu/minhook
  Version:  1.3.4 (tag v1.3.4, commit c3fcafdc10146beb5919319d0683e44e3c30d537)
  Licence:  BSD-2-Clause, including the Hacker Disassembler Engine (HDE)
            files hde32.*, hde64.*, table32.h, and table64.h. The license
            and the authors are reproduced below.
  Imported: include/MinHook.h, src/, and src/hde/, flattened into the
            above directory, with MinHook.h renamed to minhook.h and hook.c
            to minhook.c. Upstream's build files, LICENSE.txt, AUTHORS.txt,
            and src/hde/pstdint.h were left out.
  Changes:  The code was rewritten in the style of this project: the API is
            mh_*() (for example, mh_create_hook() for MH_CreateHook()), the
            HDE instruction types are struct hde32_instruction and struct
            hde64_instruction, the HDE flags are HDE32_F_* and HDE64_F_*,
            and the Win32 type aliases are replaced with the standard C
            types. HDE is built for every target (upstream only builds the
            decoder for the matching architecture) and reads its operands
            with memcpy(). Its behavior is otherwise unchanged.

            Fixes in the hooking engine:

            - mh_executable_address() no longer reads an uninitialized
              result if VirtualQuery() fails.

            - The trampoline builder refuses an x64 RIP-relative instruction
              with the address size prefix. Upstream wrote its adjusted
              displacement before the instruction copy since HDE decodes
              such an instruction without a displacement.

            The targets are declared in libcompanion/libcompanion/buildfile.

  Exception: this is a bundled dependency, contrary to the "no bundled
  dependencies" rule (charter 2.2, rule 3; [PKG] 3.5), and it was granted as
  an explicit exception on 2026-09-27.

  Reason: Companion is built for Windows (the 64-bit Steam client) and
  for Linux (the 32-bit native Steam client). On Windows it uses MinHook as a
  whole. On Linux it uses only MinHook's instruction length decoder (HDE) for
  its own detour, which requires building HDE against a stand-in for
  <windows.h>. The libminhook package on cppget.org builds for Windows only
  and doesn't expose HDE, so it can serve neither the Linux build nor the
  stand-in.

  Removal: the exception ends once a package provides HDE for every target
  Companion is built for. The vendored copy is then deleted and replaced
  with a depends: value.

  Updating: port the upstream changes since the version above into the
  rewritten code, then update the version as well as the license and the
  authors below.

